From 732b648e132ceb0a94e1549ee864eaf601371557 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 05:31:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] escha vulkan: the multi-column threshold was disabling
 speculation - decode 3.96 -> 5.60 t/s

The multi-column kernel demanded a FULL block, ncols >= 8. DFlash2 runs with n_max=3, so a verify
pass arrives with about 4 columns, fell under the threshold, and took the one-column kernel - which
re-decodes the ENTIRE weight matrix once per column. Four tokens cost four full decodes, exactly
cancelling what speculation saves.

That is what produced "speculation gains nothing on Escha" earlier today: 23/23 draft acceptance and
decode 3.14 -> 3.09. Perfect acceptance with zero speedup was the tell. It was nearly written off as
a property of the model - that the decode is weight-decompression bound and cannot be helped - and
that conclusion was wrong.

The multi-column kernel already handles a partial block: staging clamps with min(col0+cj, ncols-1)
and the store is guarded by cc < ncols, so surplus lanes recompute a column that is never written.
The real trade is ONE decode pass for a partial block against ncols passes, and it pays from
ncols=2 up. The floor stays 2 because a single column has nothing to share a decode across, which
is what the old ncols=1 measurement (C=4 1537 us vs C=1 1208 us) actually showed.

Measured on the same build, same probe shape:
    plain                    prefill 16.17   decode 3.96
    nextn-grafted + DFlash2  prefill 16.40   decode 5.60   draft 23/23
Correctness first: test-backend-ops -o ESCHA_MM, 12 OK / 0 FAIL, including the model shapes at
ncols=2 that changed path.

Found by instrumenting rather than reasoning: the drafter log prints n_max=3, and the host code
prints its own threshold. Four straight lever refutations came before this; the profiler pointed at
the generator every time, and the generator was not where the loss was.

Origin: ArifiLabs (native)
---
 ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp | 22 ++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp b/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp
index f05c0fd78..906693e3b 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp
@@ -10502,10 +10502,24 @@ static void ggml_vk_escha_mm(ggml_backend_vk_context * ctx, vk_context& subctx,
     constexpr uint32_t ESCHA_MM_COLS = 8;
     static const bool no_multicol = getenv("GGML_VK_ESCHA_NO_MULTICOL") != nullptr;
 
-    // A short op (decode, ncols=1) would leave most of a column block idle, and the wasted
-    // lanes cost real time: at ncols=1 the C=4 kernel measured 1537 us vs 1208 us for C=1 on
-    // escha2 5120x12288. Below a full block the one-column kernel is simply the better one.
-    const bool use_mc = !no_multicol && ncols >= ESCHA_MM_COLS;
+    // WHERE THE THRESHOLD BELONGS (seat-40 correction).
+    //
+    // It used to demand a FULL block, ncols >= 8. That silently disabled the whole optimisation
+    // for speculative decoding, which is the case that needs it most: DFlash2 runs with n_max=3,
+    // so a verify pass arrives with about 4 columns, took the one-column kernel, and re-decoded
+    // the entire weight matrix ONCE PER COLUMN. Four tokens cost four full decodes - precisely
+    // cancelling what speculation saves. It measured as "speculation gains nothing on Escha"
+    // (23/23 draft acceptance, decode 3.14 -> 3.09) and was very nearly written off as a property
+    // of the model.
+    //
+    // The multi-column kernel already handles a partial block correctly: staging clamps with
+    // min(col0+cj, ncols-1) and the store is guarded by cc < ncols, so the surplus lanes recompute
+    // a duplicate column that is never written. So the real trade is one decode pass for a partial
+    // block against ncols decode passes, and it pays from ncols=2 upward.
+    //
+    // The old comment's ncols=1 measurement (C=4 at 1537 us vs C=1 at 1208 us) stands and is why
+    // the floor is 2, not 1: with a single column there is nothing to share a decode across.
+    const bool use_mc = !no_multicol && ncols >= 2;
 
     // Occupancy arm (seat-40) - REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT, OFF BY DEFAULT.
     // The reasoning was that 128 threads is two waves at warp 64, too few to hide code-fetch
